Each request carries the current time, whether it originates on the hotel's on-property network, whether the underlying dispute has been verified, and whether a general-manager override has been declared.

In this workflow an adjustment is first posted on a folio and then approved, and the approval step can see the folio's status and who posted the adjustment as recorded by the posting step.

A front desk agent or night auditor may post an adjustment only when their own adjustment authority level is high enough to cover the guest's segment.

This authority requirement is waived when the folio's dispute category is one of the monitored categories such as chargeback or comp, so a front desk agent or night auditor may then post regardless of the guest's segment.

The authority requirement is also waived when the adjustment is posted before the guest's checkout deadline, so before that deadline a front desk agent or night auditor may post regardless of the guest's segment.

When neither waiver applies, meaning the dispute category is not monitored and the checkout deadline has already passed, neither a front desk agent nor a night auditor may post an adjustment for a guest whose segment outranks their authority level.

An accounting clerk or revenue manager who posted an adjustment may not approve that same adjustment, but this separation is relaxed when the approver holds the revenue-manager role required for large credits, in which case a revenue manager may approve an adjustment they posted.

A run through the workflow in which a front desk agent posts an adjustment within their authority and an accounting clerk who did not post it then approves it, tripping no prohibition above, must be able to succeed.

