Each request to access an application carries the current date, whether the requester is on the firm network, whether a court disclosure order is in effect, and whether the requester used multi-factor authentication.

A requester's clearance level must be at least as high as the confidentiality level marked on the patent application before they may access it, so a requester with only standard clearance may not open an application marked restricted.

This clearance check applies only when the requester holds the docket clerk role, since docket clerks routinely pull applications across the whole firm rather than just their own matters.

When the requester is the assigned patent attorney or the assigned examiner on that specific application, the clearance check does not apply, and they may access the application regardless of its confidentiality level.

Access is only allowed while the application is still in the pre-publication stage of its lifecycle, before it has moved to published status.

Once an application has reached published status, any of the three roles above may access it without regard to the clearance-dominance rule, because published applications are no longer confidential.

When a court disclosure order is in effect on the request, it overrides the clearance-dominance gate entirely, and access is allowed even to a requester whose clearance does not dominate the confidentiality level.

None of a patent attorney, a docket clerk, or an examiner may access a patent application whose confidentiality level exceeds their clearance while it is still in the pre-publication stage, unless a court disclosure order is in effect on that request.

When a court disclosure order is in effect, that override wins over the clearance-dominance requirement.

A request by a patent attorney to access a patent application that trips none of the prohibitions above must be able to succeed.

