Clearance levels for requesters and confidentiality levels for applications both run from standard to confidential to secret to top secret.

A patent attorney, a docket clerk, or an examiner may access a patent application only when their clearance level is at or above that application's confidentiality level.

A requester whose clearance level is below the application's confidentiality level must not be granted access, no matter which of these roles they hold.

A patent attorney may access a patent application only when they are the attorney of record for that application.

A patent attorney who is not the attorney of record for an application must not access it, even when their clearance level is sufficient.

A docket clerk may access a patent application only when they are listed on that application's docket team.

A docket clerk who is not on an application's docket team must not access it, even when their clearance level is sufficient.

An examiner may access a patent application only when they are that application's assigned examiner, which also means an examiner who is also the inventor on a different application gains no access rights from that overlap.

An examiner who is not the assigned examiner for an application must not access it, even when their clearance level is sufficient.

A patent attorney, a docket clerk, and an examiner must each be denied access to any patent application whenever the specific condition required for their own role is not met.

A request by the attorney of record for a patent application, whose clearance level meets that application's confidentiality level, to access that application must be able to succeed.

