## Access-Control Requirement
The system manages the dispatch of carrier shipments and the confirmation of their delivery.

The people involved are planners, drivers, dispatch managers, depot supervisors, and auditors. One person may hold both the planner and the dispatch manager role at the same time.

The resources are shipments and delivery receipts. Every shipment records its planner, its driver of record, its piece count, its gross weight in kilograms, its status, its tracking code, its origin depot, its hazmat class, and the end of its delivery window. Every delivery receipt records its shipment, its recipient, and when it was signed.

Each request carries the current time, whether it comes from the depot network, whether an expedite has been declared, and whether proof of delivery is present.

In this workflow a shipment is first dispatched and then its delivery is confirmed, and the confirmation step can see the shipment's status and who dispatched it as the driver of record as recorded by the dispatch step.

A driver or a dispatch manager may dispatch a shipment.

The person who planned a shipment may not be its driver of record who dispatches it, and a driver of record who did not plan the shipment may dispatch it.

A shipment may be dispatched only within its delivery window, before the delivery window ends, and a dispatch after the window has closed is refused.

When a shipment carries a hazmat classification tag, its dispatch is subject to the additional hazmat handling requirement, and a shipment without such a tag needs no hazmat handling.

A driver or a depot supervisor may confirm a delivery, but the same driver of record who dispatched the shipment must be the one to confirm its delivery, and anyone else may not confirm it.

Neither a driver nor a dispatch manager who planned a shipment may dispatch it as its driver of record.

A run through the workflow begun by a driver who did not plan the shipment, dispatching it within its delivery window and then confirming its delivery, tripping no prohibition above, must be able to succeed.

